<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shahaji Bhonsle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1594">1594</a>–<a href="/years/1664">1664</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Shahaji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shahaji Bhonsle was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> general in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>, founder of the Maratha kingdom. He served the Ahmadnagar Sultanate and, after its fall to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, briefly propped up a puppet Nizam Shah until he surrendered in 1636. He then entered the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a>, holding estates around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shahaji-bhonsle.jpg" alt="Shahaji Bhonsle" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: अज्ञात · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shahajiraje_Bhosale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shahaji was born on 15 March 1594 to Maloji Bhosale, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> nobleman who held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> and Supe and other lands from Sultan Murtuza Nizamshah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>. As a child he was betrothed to Jijabai, daughter of Lakhuji Jadhav, the Deshmukh of Sindkhed. He served in the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ambar">Malik Ambar</a>, prime minister of the Ahmadnagar Sultanate, and was a minor commander when his father died in 1622. By 1625 he held the rank of Sar Lashkar, major general.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> and the Mughal Empire Shahaji changed sides several times: to Bijapur in 1625, back to Ahmadnagar in 1628 and to the Mughals in 1630, after his in-laws were murdered in the factional politics of the Ahmadnagar court. In 1632 the Mughals gave his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ambar">Malik Ambar</a>'s son Fatah Khan, and Shahaji left them. When the Mughals captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Daulatabad</a> that year he held a war-torn area between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> and Ahmadnagar, set up the young Murtaza as king with himself as chief minister, and raised an army that at its height numbered 12,000.</p><p>Defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paranda">Parenda</a> in 1634 and driven back when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> came to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> in person, he surrendered Mahuli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junnar">Junnar</a> in October 1636 under the Mughal-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> treaty. Barred from living in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> jagir, which his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> held in name under the manager Dadoji Kondadev, Shahaji served Bijapur in the south. Bijapur's forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a> in December 1638 and gave it to him as a jagir, and he ruled the region almost independently, building gardens and a palace called Gowri Mahal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>'s government rose and fell. In 1648, while Bijapur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> besieged Jinjee, he was arrested for dealing on his own with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayakas</a>, brought to Bijapur in chains and made to give up Kondana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a>, but he was pardoned within a year. Most historians believe he did not support <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>'s rising. Between 1659 and 1662 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> as a mediator, meeting his son for the first time in 12 years. He died in a hunting accident and is buried at Hodigere, near Channagiri in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shahaji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bijapur annexed Bidar in 1619, and in the 1630s and 1640s its generals, among them the Maratha commander <b>Shahaji Bhonsle</b>, conquered much of the southern Karnataka plateau and parts of the Tamil country, taking Bangalore in 1638 and the fortress of Gingee in 1649.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The founder, Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj (1630–1680), son of the Bijapur commander <b>Shahaji Bhonsle</b>, used the rugged terrain of the Western Ghats and his grasp of guerrilla warfare to seize strategically placed forts such as Torna, Rajgad and later Raigad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Shahaji Bhonsle</b>, a Maratha general, propped up a puppet Nizam Shah until Shah Jahan forced his surrender in 1636 and annexed the remaining territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/ahmadnagar-sultanate">Ahmadnagar Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Pratap Singh governed with energy, building roads and public works, but the British deposed him in 1839 on charges of intrigue and installed his brother <b>Shahaji</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/satara-state">Satara State</a></li></ul></section>
